DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     hhoosstt is a simple utility for performing DNS lookups.  It is normally used
     to convert names to IP addresses and vice versa.

     _n_a_m_e is the domain name that is to be looked up.  It can also be a dot‐
     ted-decimal IPv4 address or a colon-delimited IPv6 address, in which case
     hhoosstt will by default perform a reverse lookup for that address.

     When _n_a_m_e is not provided, hhoosstt prints a short summary of it's usage.

     _s_e_r_v_e_r is an optional argument which is either a domain name or an IP ad‐
     dress of the name server that hhoosstt should query instead of the server or
     servers listed in _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f.  When _s_e_r_v_e_r is a domain name, system
     resolver is used to obtain it's address.

     Supported options:

     --aa      Make a verbose query of type AANNYY.  Equivalent to --vv --tt AANNYY.

     --CC      Query for SSOOAA records for zone _n_a_m_e from all of it's authorita‐
             tive name servers.  The list of name servers is obtained via NNSS
             query for _n_a_m_e.

     --cc _c_l_a_s_s
             Perform DNS query of class _c_l_a_s_s.  Recognized classes are IINN
             (Internet), CCHH (Chaosnet), HHSS (Hesiod), NNOONNEE, AANNYY and CCLLAASSSS_N
             (where _N is a number from 1 to 255).  Default is IINN.

     --dd      Produce verbose output.  This is a synonym for --vv, and is pro‐
             vided for backward compatibility.

     --ii      Use IP6.INT domain for reverse lookups of IPv6 addresses (as de‐
             fined in RFC1886; note that RFC4159 deprecates IP6.INT).  By de‐
             fault IP6.ARPA is used.

     --ll      List all NNSS,, PPTTRR,, AA and AAAAAAAA records in zone _n_a_m_e by performing a
             zone transfer (AAXXFFRR).  You can combine this option with --aa to
             print all records, or with --tt to only print specific ones.

     --NN _n_d_o_t_s
             Consider names with at least this many dots as absolute.  That
             is, try to resolve them directly before consulting ddoommaaiinn or
             sseeaarrcchh options from _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f.

     --rr      Perform non-recursive query to the name server by clearing RD
             (“recursion desired”) bit of the query.

     --RR _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Retry this many times when a query does not receive an answer in
             time.  The default is 1 retry.  If _n_u_m_b_e_r is negative or zero, 1
             is used instead.

     --ss      Report SERVFAIL responses as they are, do not ignore them.

     --TT      Query name server over TCP.  By default UDP is used, except for
             AAXXFFRR and IIXXFFRR queries, which require TCP.  hhoosstt will also retry
             UDP queries in TCP mode if the UDP response was truncated (i.e.
             had TC bit set).

     --tt _t_y_p_e
             Perform DNS query of type _t_y_p_e, which can be any standard query
             type name (AA, CCNNAAMMEE, MMXX, TTXXTT, etc), a wildcard query (AANNYY), or
             TTYYPPEE_N, where _N is a number from 1 to 65535.  For IIXXFFRR
             (incremental zone transfer) queries the starting serial number
             can be specified by appending an equal sign followed by the num‐
             ber (e.g. --tt IIXXFFRR=12345678).

             The default is to query for AA, AAAAAAAA, and MMXX records, unless --CC or
             --ll options are given (in which case SSOOAA or AAXXFFRR queries are made)
             or _n_a_m_e is a valid IP address (in which case reverse lookup using
             PPTTRR query is performed).

     --vv      Produce verbose output.

     --ww      Wait forever (or for a very long time) for response from the name
